.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 226 PM PDT Mon Oct 26 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

Seasonal to seasonally cool temperatures continue through the
upcoming week as upper level troughing lingers over the West Coast.
Overnight temperatures will also be on the cooler side with morning
lows dropping into the 40s to upper 30s across the region. In the
Short Term, expect more classic fall weather to continue with
gustier onshore winds in the afternoon/evening. Stronger winds peak
between 25 to 30 mph along the coast and across mountain gaps/passes
(Altamont Pass, Salinas Valley, San Bruno Gap - to name a few).
Winds ease overnight but expect another round of gusty onshore winds
during the afternoon/evening on Tuesday.

The main hazard Monday/Tuesday continues to be the threat for
coastal flooding along the San Francisco/San Pablo Bay shorelines
and along the Pacific Coast. This coastal flood event is brought to
you by a combination of the full moon (10/26) and thermal expansion
due to warmer ocean temperatures on top of our typical tidal
pattern. High tides peak today and tomorrow with up to 2.1 ft of
inundation above ground level expected. The strongest impacts will
be felt along low-lying portions of bayshore Marin County where
numerous flooding reports have already been received. If you live in
a low-lying area prone to flooding along the bay or Pacific Coast,
make sure to have all flood preparations in place before high tide
tomorrow (12:15 PM). If you encounter a flooded road, turn around
don`t drown. You don`t know how deep flood waters are, how fast a
current may be, or if the road is structurally sound beneath the
water. Coastal flooding is set to peak on Tuesday with tidal heights
dropping throughout the long term (for more in depth discussion see
the Long Term forecast).

