.LONG TERM...
Issued at 226 PM PDT Mon Oct 26 2026
(Wednesday through next Sunday)

Troughing generally persists through the upcoming week with a deeper
upper level low arriving Friday into the weekend. Seasonal to
seasonally cool temperatures continue through the extended forecast
but expect temperatures to drop another couple of degrees Friday
into the weekend. Diurnally gusty winds continue for favored
locations this week with stronger gusts during the afternoon/evening
along the coast and across mountain gaps/passes. High temperatures
Wednesday and Thursday will be in the mid to upper 60s across the
interior while coastal areas linger in the upper 50s to low 60s.
Friday we see the promised further cooldown with interior lows
dropping into the low 60s and most coastal areas lingering in the
mid to upper 50s. Cooler temperatures remain in place through the
end of the weekend. Expect overnight cloud cover to return by
midweek with cloudy skies becoming more predominant Thursday into
the weekend as the deeper trough arrives. Light rain remains
possible Friday into the weekend but, fortunately, the bulk of the
rain has shifted southwards towards Southern California. That`s not
to say we will be completely dry this weekend. Expect drizzly
conditions across the Bay Area Saturday into Sunday with light rain
possible overnight heading into Sunday. Light rain is expected
across the Central Coast but totals should be fairly light across
urban areas (around a tenth of an inch). For the higher elevations
of the Santa Lucia Range and the Gabilan Range, light rain amounting
to a couple tenths of an inch is possible. All together, the classic
fall weather should lend itself a ghostly ambiance for anyone
partaking in any haunted/spooktacular activities this weekend.

Coastal flooding concerns continue across Bayshore locations within
the San Pablo, San Francisco, and Monterey Bays and the Pacific
coastal regions through at least Thursday. While tidal heights peak
Tuesday, heights will remain elevated through late week. At the San
Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.9 ft above
normal (7.7 ft MLLW) at 12:56 PM Wednesday 10/28, 1.7 ft above
normal (7.5 ft MLLW) at 01:43 PM Thursday 10/29, 1.4 ft above normal
(7.2 ft MLLW) at 2:39 PM on Friday 10/30, and 1.0 feet above normal
(6.8 ft MLLW) at 3:44 PM on Saturday 10/31, including around 1.0 ft
of surge residual partially attributed to impacts from El Nino.
Currently, tidal heights continue to run above the forecast with the
forecast heights for Monday/Tuesday being increased. If this trend
continues into mid to late week we may see coastal flooding concerns
extended Friday into the weekend (necessitating the extension of the
product). Expect the worst flooding to continue in low-lying
bayshore locations of Marin County.

